# KSV-0109 ("ConfigMap with secrets") fires on charts/preauth-proxy/values.yaml's default `config`,
# whose credential body carries the literal `password=${CRED_SECRET}`. That is an unresolved
# `${ENV}` reference, substituted by the binary from its environment at request time, never a
# value stored in the ConfigMap (see the inline note on that line and docs/bricks/preauth-proxy.md's
# *Secrets*) — so the finding is a false positive, and without this entry `task audit` and
# `trivy fs` fail on it. Plain rule-ID form, not path-scoped: this
# trivy version's structured `misconfigurations: [{id, paths}]` ignore format did not suppress
# the finding even when the path matched exactly (tested against the same target `trivy fs`
# actually scans), so this is repo-wide for this one rule. Revisit if a second ConfigMap or
# Secret ever legitimately needs KSV-0109 to fire — there is only the one false positive today.
KSV-0109

# KSV-0056 ("manage networking resources") fires on charts/weebo-si-operator/templates/rbac.yaml's
# controller ClusterRole, which writes `networkpolicies` (and, behind their own values,
# `ingresses` patch, `services`/`endpointslices` for the OpenShift dialect). That write is the
# brick's whole job — RFC 0004's *Security considerations* argues the grant and how it is bounded
# (ownership label, namespace exclusions, the diff), and the chart comments repeat it inline. Same
# repo-wide caveat as KSV-0109 above: revisit if any other chart ever grants networking writes.
KSV-0056
